SPOILER ALERT


Enchant weapon scroll:

A blessed scroll will increase a weapon's enchantment by 3.  An uncursed
scroll will increase it by 1.  A cursed one will decrease it by 1.  If a
weapon starts out at > +5 (i.e., is currently +6 or more) when you read a
blessed or uncursed enchant weapon scroll, there is a 2/3 chance of it
evaporating.  This works similiarly, but in reverse, for disenchanting.
If a weapon starts out at < -5 when you read a cursed enchant weapon
scroll, there is a 2/3 change that it will evaporate, too.

Kaoru Maeda came up with a great idea for getting to +8 safely if your
weapon is currently +6 or +7.  Engrave with it (and thus dull it) until
the it is down to +5.  Now you can read a blessed enchant weapon scroll,
and get it to +8 without risk.

Bottom line:  If it's +5 or less, you can use a blessed enchant weapon
scroll to get to +8 with no risk.  If it's +6 or +7, try Kaoru Maeda's
trick.  If it's +8 or more, leave it alone!!!


Enchant armor scroll:

A blessed scroll will increase a piece of armor's enchantment by a random
amount of from 1 to 3.  An uncursed scroll will increase it by 1.  A cursed
one will decrease it by 1.  If the armor is > +3 (> +5 for elven mithril)
when you read a blessed or uncursed enchant armor scroll, there is a
(1 - (1/current enchantment)) chance of it evaporating.  Thus, for example,
if it's currently +4, there's a 75% chance of it evaporating; if it's +5,
there's an 80% change of losing it; and so on.

Along the same line as Kaoru Maeda's trick for weapons, if your piece of
armor is not rustproof (see WCST file) and is currently, say, +4, then,
first, do something to reduce it to +3.  For instance, kick an acid blob
while wearing only that piece of armor, and (hopefully) have it corrode
from splashed acid.  Now read the blessed enchant armor scroll.  You may
only go back up to +4, but, then again, you could get +5 or +6.  However,
it would probably be better to enchant something else with the scroll,
or use it for rustproofing.

Bottom line:  If it's +3 or less (+5 or less for elven mithril), you can
use a blessed enchant armor scroll with no risk.  If it's +4 or more (+6
or more for elven mithril), leave it, and enchant something else, or use
it for rustproofing.


Note:  A character's "luck" does not factor into determining the
probability of evaporation (or the amount of enchantment for a blessed
enchant armor scroll).  You're strictly at the mercy of the pseudo-random
number generator.


